WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.692
Prompts and outputs should be treated as potentially sensitive data.

00:00:03.692 --> 00:00:08.002
They may contain user identifiers, private documents, source code, secrets, business plans,

00:00:08.002 --> 00:00:10.876
regulated content, customer records, or internal operational details.

00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:15.545
Even if the model service is private, the surrounding logs, analytics, debug traces,

00:00:15.545 --> 00:00:19.137
prompt history, and storage systems can become data exposure paths.

00:00:19.137 --> 00:00:21.652
Log minimization is a practical privacy control.

00:00:21.652 --> 00:00:24.885
Capture enough information to operate the service, investigate errors,

00:00:24.885 --> 00:00:26.321
and support audit requirements,

00:00:26.321 --> 00:00:30.632
but avoid storing full prompts and outputs unless there is a clear

00:00:30.632 --> 00:00:32.787
need and an approved retention model.

00:00:32.787 --> 00:00:37.456
Redaction can help at a high level, but it is not a guarantee.

00:00:37.456 --> 00:00:39.971
Sensitive values may appear in unexpected forms,

00:00:39.971 --> 00:00:43.563
and overly broad debug logging can defeat careful API design.

00:00:43.563 --> 00:00:47.514
Retention policies should define what is stored, where it is stored,

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:50.747
who can access it, and when it is deleted.

00:00:50.747 --> 00:00:54.339
Prompt history may be valuable for user experience or troubleshooting,

00:00:54.339 --> 00:00:56.494
but it also creates privacy obligations.

00:00:56.494 --> 00:01:00.804
Local storage, cloud storage, backups, and analytics pipelines should all be considered.

00:01:00.804 --> 00:01:05.115
Data copied into secondary systems can outlive the original request unless deletion

00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:06.911
and retention are designed intentionally.

00:01:06.911 --> 00:01:10.503
Privacy expectations should be communicated to application owners and users.

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.658
If prompts are logged, say so.

00:01:12.658 --> 00:01:16.609
If outputs are retained for review, define who can review them.

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:21.638
If data is sent to a hosted provider, understand provider terms and organizational policy.

00:01:21.638 --> 00:01:25.948
A well-run LLM API does not rely on vague assumptions about privacy.

00:01:25.948 --> 00:01:29.540
It defines data handling as part of the service contract.
